#!/bin/bash
#
# check_shelly - Nagios/Icinga plugin for Shelly Gen2+ RPC devices
#                (developed against a Shelly Power Strip Gen4, S4PL-00416EU)
#
# --power    Per-socket power draw (apower, W) and current (A) via
#            Switch.GetStatus. Warns/criticals when draw exceeds a
#            configurable wattage -- useful for catching an overloaded
#            socket or an appliance stuck drawing far more than normal.
#            Every requested socket is reported, on or off, so the
#            perfdata trend is continuous even while a socket is idle.
#            Also honours --expect-on/--expect-off (see --state below)
#            for whichever sockets are in scope for this mode (--socket,
#            default: all) -- e.g. assert socket 3 draws <1800W AND is on.
#
# --voltage  Per-socket mains voltage. Nominal EU mains is 230V; default
#            thresholds flag a brown-out or surge at roughly +/-5%/+/-10%
#            (warn 218-242V / crit 207-253V) -- override with --warn-low
#            et al. if local grid tolerances differ. Also honours
#            --expect-on/--expect-off like --power does.
#
# --wifi     Wi-Fi signal strength (RSSI, dBm) via Shelly.GetStatus.
#            Also goes CRITICAL if wifi.status isn't "got ip" (device
#            not actually on the network, independent of signal quality).
#
# --health   Device-level housekeeping: sys.restart_required, a pending
#            firmware update (sys.available_updates non-empty), and how
#            long it's been since the last successful time sync
#            (sys.last_sync_ts) -- a device that can't reach NTP drifts,
#            and its aenergy/by_minute timestamps become unreliable.
#
# --state    Asserts specific sockets are on and/or specific sockets are
#            off (e.g. a server/NAS socket that must never be off, or a
#            socket that must stay off for safety). Severity is settable
#            per list: --expect-on/--expect-off escalate to CRITICAL,
#            --expect-on-warn/--expect-off-warn only to WARNING -- there's
#            no single right answer for which direction (on-when-should-
#            be-off vs. off-when-should-be-on) matters more, it depends
#            what's plugged into the socket, so it's a choice per socket
#            rather than a fixed rule. Unlisted sockets are not checked.
#            All four lists also work with --power/--voltage (folded
#            into that mode's own result), so a single check can assert
#            both a threshold and a state, e.g. "socket 3 must be on and
#            draw under 1800W".
#
# All modes authenticate with HTTP Digest (SHA-256), which is what
# Shelly Gen2/3/4 devices require once a device password is set. RPC
# reference: https://shelly-api-docs.shelly.cloud/gen2/
